Yes. And that reframes history from a collection of past events into a continuously propagating causal process.

There isn’t really:

\text{Past}\;|\;\text{Present}\;|\;\text{Future}

except as useful segmentation. There is a continuous sequence:

\cdots\rightarrow S_{t-2}\rightarrow S_{t-1}\rightarrow
\boxed{S_t}
\rightarrow S_{t+1}\rightarrow\cdots

We’re simply standing inside S_t and calling it now.

That connects almost everything from this thread.

The prehistoric person whose name disappeared isn’t disconnected from us merely because their story wasn’t recorded. Their actions entered subsequent states. Some consequences vanished; others propagated through descendants, language, technology, institutions, landscapes, traditions, selection pressures, migrations, and countless invisible causal chains.

So history has at least three forms of continuation:

Recorded continuation — we know the chain.

Inferred continuation — consequences survive strongly enough that we can reconstruct portions of the chain.

Latent continuation — the causal influence remains, but its origin has become unrecoverable.

That last category is enormous.

X\rightarrow A\rightarrow B\rightarrow C\rightarrow\boxed{\text{Us}}

We may know A,B,C while X has disappeared completely.

Yet X still participated in producing the present.

Which gives a striking definition:

\boxed{\text{The present is history's current state.}}

And we’re simultaneously its consequence and next cause.

That means every present action has two faces:

\text{Action}_{now}
=
\text{consequence of prior sequences}
+
\text{input to future sequences}

That’s the continuation.

It also means history doesn’t suddenly become fundamentally different because we have smartphones, AI, satellites, or modern institutions. Those are newly available states in the same unfolding process. Humans still inherit, prioritize, cooperate, compete, love, build, tell stories, forget, rediscover, transmit, distort, adapt, and leave afterglow.

And eventually our extraordinarily vivid present will look like antiquity.

Most conversations gone.
Most people reduced to a few records or none.
Some enormous contemporary concerns forgotten.
Some seemingly trivial decisions unexpectedly consequential.

Future people will reconstruct us from incomplete evidence and wonder what they’re missing.

Which completes the symmetry with prehistory:

\boxed{
\text{Lost Past}
\rightarrow
\text{Us}
\rightarrow
\text{Partially Lost Future-Past}
}

We’re not standing after history looking backward at it.

We’re one of the parts currently happening.